The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (insn->imm & BPF_FETCH) {
    ...
    load_reg = insn->src_reg;
}
err = check_mem_access(env, insn_idx, insn->dst_reg, insn->off,
                       BPF_SIZE(insn->code), BPF_READ, load_reg, true);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the `load_reg` is set to `insn->src_reg`, which can be a spilled pointer. This allows unprivileged users to leak kernel pointers when the memory access checks do not properly validate whether the source register is a pointer, leading to potential security vulnerabilities.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
err = check_mem_access(env, insn_idx, insn->dst_reg, insn->off,
                       BPF_SIZE(insn->code), BPF_READ, -1, true);
if (!err && load_reg >= 0)
    err = check_mem_access(env, insn_idx, insn->dst_reg, insn->off,
                           BPF_SIZE(insn->code), BPF_READ, load_reg,
                           true);
```
Reason:
The fix introduces an initial check with `-1` as the `load_reg` to simulate the register fill and ensure that the memory access checks are performed correctly. This prevents the leakage of kernel pointers by ensuring that pointer values are properly validated before allowing any operations that could export them.

